// A fallible ctor on a GENERIC instance, used inline as a `match` subject. This was ROADMAP row 2, and it
// was TWO defects in series — which is why deleting either one alone measurably changed nothing:
//
//   1. DISCOVERY. `G::<int32>.open(…)` written as a match subject was never walked (no `MatchNode` arm in
//      `scanExprForGenerics`), so `G<int32>` was never registered and `_classes.find(inst)` missed. The
//      workaround was to mention `G::<int32>` somewhere else in the same function, which is a site that
//      IS walked.
//   2. RENDERING. With the instance registered, `exprClass`'s dot-on-type branch then bailed on
//      `if (inst != dotTy) return "";` — a fallible ctor's declared `Result<G<T>, OpenErr>` needs the owning
//      instance's type args bound to render. `cTypeInInstance` binds exactly those.
//
// The infallible ctor on the same instance always worked, which is what made the row read as a
// fallible-ctor problem rather than a discovery one.
type enum OpenErr implements Error {
    Bad;

    public const fn string message() { return "bad"; }
}
type value G<T> {
    public T item;
    public ctor Result<G<T>, OpenErr> open(T item) {
        this.item = item;
        return Result::Ok(value: this);
    }
    public ctor make(T item) { this.item = item; }
}
fn int32 main()
{
    int32 a = match (G::<int32>.open(item: 40)) { case Ok(value: v): v.item; case Err(error: e): 0; };  // 40
    int32 b = G::<int32>.make(item: 2).item;                                                            // 2
    return a + b;   // 42
}
